## MKV

Primary dataset.

Approximately:

* 73k products
* 19 major categories
* many subcategories / third-level categories
* heterogeneous DIY/home/building catalogue
* brand present for many but not all products
* model often missing
* no reliable barcode field
* important identity/package information frequently exists only in title or metadata

MKV contains many different measurement semantics.

Examples include:

```text
3.9 x 25 mm, 1000 vnt.
2.5 L
150 x 70 cm
750 ml x 12 vnt.
```

These values cannot all be interpreted the same way.

MKV should drive discovery of the canonical structure and normalization rules.

---

## SNK

Approximately 11k+ products.

Generally more structured than MKV.

Useful characteristics include:

* strong brand coverage
* barcode availability
* structured metadata
* explicit package-related information for many products

SNK can therefore act both as:

1. another source to normalize into the common representation
2. a useful validation source for some title-derived values

For example:

```text
title: "... 250 vnt."
structured package field: 250
```

Comparing independently available signals can provide measurable normalization-quality metrics.

---

## TOP

Approximately 1k+ products.

More standardized electronics/appliance-oriented data.

Characteristics include strong:

* brand information
* model information
* barcode information

TOP should help verify that the normalized structure is not unnecessarily tied to MKV-specific assumptions.

---

## BNU

Approximately 21k+ pharmacy products across Baltic markets.

BNU is a different product domain but much more standardized.

Typical useful fields include:

* manufacturer
* barcode
* form
* active substance
* strength
* amount in package

Example title:

```text
Paracetamol 500 mg N20
```

Semantically:

```text
500 mg = product strength
N20    = package count
```

The normalization model must not confuse these concepts.

BNU is useful as a final test that the common structure can support another domain without redesigning the whole process.

